        <div id="gauge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 15 vs 16 Gauge Finish Nailer: 16 Gauge Suits Most Trim, 15 Gauge Holds Doors and Heavy Casing
          </h2>

          <p>
            A finish nailer drives 15- or 16-gauge nails, usually 1 to 2-1/2 inches long. The gauge is the wire thickness, and smaller numbers mean thicker nails. That one choice decides how well the trim holds and how big a hole you fill afterwards.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">16 Gauge: The All-Rounder</h3>

          <p>
            Sixteen-gauge nails are thin enough to leave a small hole and to avoid splitting narrow trim, but strong enough for baseboard, window casing, chair rail, cabinets and most crown. Straight 16-gauge nails are also the easiest finish nails to buy. If you are trimming a typical house and want one tool, 16-gauge is the safe choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">15 Gauge: Maximum Holding Power</h3>

          <p>
            Fifteen-gauge nails are the thickest finish nails. They hold heavy door jambs, thick casing, stair treads and solid hardwood trim more securely, and they are better at pulling a slightly bowed board tight to the wall. The cost is a bigger hole to fill and more risk of splitting thin stock. Every 15-gauge finish nailer uses an angled magazine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">18 Gauge Is a Brad Nailer, Not a Finish Nailer</h3>

          <p>
            If your work is mostly small moldings, shoe mold, picture frames and cabinet backs, you may want an 18-gauge brad nailer instead. Brads leave the smallest hole but hold much less. Our <a href="/best-brad-nailer/">best brad nailer guide</a> covers those tools, and many trim carpenters own both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Framing Nailer Takes Over</h3>

          <p>
            Finish nailers are not for structural work. Wall framing, decks and sheathing need the much larger nails of a <a href="/best-framing-nailer/">framing nailer</a>, which drives clipped or round-head nails up to about 3-1/2 inches. A finish nailer should never be used where the fastener carries a structural load.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="angled-vs-straight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Angled vs Straight Finish Nailer: Angled Reaches Corners, Straight Nails Are Easier to Find
          </h2>

          <p>
            The magazine angle is the second big decision. It changes where the tool fits and which nails you can buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Angled Finish Nailers</h3>

          <p>
            An angled magazine tilts back from the nose, so the tool can get into inside corners, against door stops and up near the ceiling when nailing crown. That is why every 15-gauge finish nailer is angled, and why the DeWalt DCN650B, Metabo HPT NT65MA4 and Milwaukee M18 FUEL 16-gauge model in this guide all use one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Straight Finish Nailers</h3>

          <p>
            A straight magazine sticks out at a right angle from the nose. It is a little harder to use in corners, but straight 16-gauge nails are the cheapest and most widely stocked finish nails. For baseboard and casing on open walls, a straight nailer like the DeWalt DCN662B or Bostitch BTFP71917 works perfectly well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Collation Before You Buy Nails</h3>

          <p>
            Angled nails are not all the same. Fifteen-gauge angled nails come in DA-style and FN-style collations, which use different angles and are not interchangeable. The Metabo HPT NT65MA4 lists DA-style nails, while Bostitch makes FN-style 15-gauge tools. Always match the nail style and angle to the tool's manual.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cordless-vs-pneumatic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cordless or Pneumatic Finish Nailer? Cordless for Most DIYers, Air for Whole Houses
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery nailers have improved enough that most homeowners should start with cordless. Pneumatic still wins for full-time trim carpenters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cordless: Pick It Up and Nail</h3>

          <p>
            A cordless finish nailer has no compressor to start and no hose to drag across finished floors. For a door casing, a loose baseboard or a weekend of trim, that saves more time than any difference in firing speed. The trade-offs are weight and, on some models, a short spin-up before each shot. Newer designs like the Metabo HPT NT1865DSA list no ramp-up time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pneumatic: Lighter and Faster on Big Jobs</h3>

          <p>
            A pneumatic nailer is lighter in the hand, fires instantly and rarely jams on long runs. The Metabo HPT NT65MA4 is listed at 4.2 lbs, much lighter than a cordless 15-gauge tool with its battery. If you already own a compressor or are trimming a whole house, air is still the professional standard. See our <a href="/best-air-compressor/">air compressor guide</a> if you need one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Battery You Already Own</h3>

          <p>
            For cordless, the platform matters more than small spec differences. A bare DeWalt or Milwaukee nailer is the cheapest route if you own those batteries, and our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt comparison</a> explains how the two platforms differ. If you own neither, a complete kit like the Metabo HPT NT1865DSA avoids buying batteries separately.
          </p>

