        <div id="uses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is a Finish Nailer Used For? Baseboard, Casing, Crown and Built-Ins
          </h2>

          <p>
            A finish nailer is for any trim that needs to stay put and be filled and painted over. These are the jobs it handles best.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Baseboard</h3>

          <p>
            Baseboard is the most common finish nailer job. Nail into the bottom plate near the floor and into studs higher up. A 16-gauge tool is plenty for most baseboard; thick hardwood base benefits from 15-gauge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Door and Window Casing</h3>

          <p>
            Casing is nailed to the jamb with shorter nails and to the framing with longer ones. Heavy exterior doors and jambs are where 15-gauge earns its place, because the casing takes knocks every time the door slams.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Crown Molding</h3>

          <p>
            Crown is nailed at an angle into the top plate and studs, often overhead. An angled magazine helps the nose reach close to the ceiling, and a lighter tool makes long runs less tiring.
          </p>

          <p>
            On outside corners, cope or miter the joint first, then pin the miter with a small nail through the joint so it does not open as the house moves through the seasons. A finish nailer is too heavy for that pin; a brad nailer is the better tool there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cabinets, Stairs and Built-Ins</h3>

          <p>
            Face frames, shelving, stair skirts and treads all suit a finish nailer. On cabinets, 16-gauge is less likely to split thin stock; on stair treads, 15-gauge holds better and reduces squeaks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nail-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Size Nails Does a Finish Nailer Use? About Three Times the Trim Thickness
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most finish nailers take nails from about 1 to 2-1/2 inches. The Metabo HPT NT1865DSA lists 1 to 2-1/2 inches; the NT65MA4 and Bostitch BTFP71917 list 1-1/4 to 2-1/2 inches. Choosing the right length within that range matters more than most people think.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rule of Thumb</h3>

          <p>
            A common trim carpentry rule is that the nail should be roughly three times as long as the trim is thick, or long enough to pass through the trim and drywall and still bite about an inch into framing. Too short and the trim loosens; too long and you risk hitting pipes or wires.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Typical Lengths by Job</h3>

          <p>
            Thin casing nailed to a jamb usually takes shorter nails, around 1-1/4 to 1-1/2 inches. Baseboard and casing nailed through drywall into studs usually need 2 to 2-1/2 inches. Crown depends on its size and the blocking behind it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hardwood trim and MDF behave differently too. Dense oak and maple resist nails, so a slightly longer nail and a deeper depth setting help; MDF is soft at the face but can mushroom around the nail head, so keep the depth just below flush and avoid nailing too close to the edge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Two Lengths on Hand</h3>

          <p>
            Most trim jobs need a short nail and a long nail. Buying both up front saves trips to the store halfway through a door.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="features" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Finish Nailer Features That Actually Matter: Depth, Nose and Firing Mode
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every finish nailer drives nails. The features below are what separate a tool that leaves clean work from one that leaves dents to fill.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tool-Free Depth Adjustment</h3>

          <p>
            You want each nail set just below the surface, ready for filler. Most picks in this guide list tool-free depth adjustment. Test on scrap of the same material and adjust before nailing real trim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Narrow Nose and No-Mar Tip</h3>

          <p>
            A small nose lets you see where the nail will land. DeWalt lists a micro nose on the DCN650B and DCN662B, and Bostitch a Smart Point nose. A no-mar pad keeps the tip from denting soft or painted trim.
          </p>

          <p>
            A built-in LED light, a belt hook and an on-tool nail-size guide are small conveniences, but none of them changes the finished work. Prioritize depth, nose and weight over extras when comparing two tools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sequential and Bump Firing</h3>

          <p>
            Sequential mode fires one nail per trigger pull and is safer and more precise. Bump or contact mode fires each time the nose is pressed while the trigger is held, which is faster on long runs. A selectable trigger, listed on most picks here, gives you both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Jam Release and Dry-Fire Lockout</h3>

          <p>
            Tool-free jam release means a bent nail costs seconds, not minutes. A dry-fire lockout stops the tool firing when the magazine is empty, which protects both the driver and your trim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Use a Finish Nailer for Clean, Tight Trim
          </h2>

          <p>
            Good trim is more about technique than tool. These steps work with any finish nailer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Find the Framing</h3>

          <p>
            Mark studs and plates before you start. Nails into framing hold; nails into drywall alone do not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Set Depth on Scrap</h3>

          <p>
            Fire a few nails into an offcut of the same trim. Adjust until heads sit just below the surface without crushing the wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Hold the Nose Square and Firm</h3>

          <p>
            Press the nose firmly against the trim and keep it square to the surface, unless you are deliberately angling into framing for crown or a corner. A loose grip lets the tool bounce and leaves a proud nail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep nails at least half an inch from board ends and an inch from the top edge of thin trim, where splitting is most likely. On hardwood, a dab of glue on a miter joint plus two nails holds better than four nails alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Fill and Finish</h3>

          <p>
            Fill nail holes with a good filler, let it dry, sand lightly and paint. Our <a href="/best-wood-filler/">wood filler guide</a> covers paintable and stainable options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Finish Nailer Not Sinking Nails? Check Depth, Power and the Nails
          </h2>

          <p>
            When nails stand proud or bend, the cause is usually simple.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Depth Setting Too Shallow</h3>

          <p>
            The most common cause. Turn the depth adjuster deeper in small steps and test on scrap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Low Air Pressure or a Tired Battery</h3>

          <p>
            On a pneumatic tool, check the regulator and hose length; long, thin hoses lose pressure. On a cordless tool, a low or cold battery can reduce driving power, so swap to a fresh pack and keep batteries warm in winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pneumatic nailers also need a few drops of tool oil in the air inlet unless they are oil-free like the Bostitch BTFP71917. A dry driver can slow down and leave nails proud, so follow the manual and oil a pneumatic nailer at the start of each day it is used.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wrong Nails or Hard Material</h3>

          <p>
            Nails with the wrong angle or collation can misfeed and jam. Dense hardwoods may need a deeper setting, a longer nail or a 15-gauge tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Holding the Tool Loosely</h3>

          <p>
            If the nose lifts off the work as the tool fires, nails will not seat. Keep firm, steady pressure through each shot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brad-vs-finish" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Finish Nailer vs Brad Nailer: Buy the Finish Nailer First for Whole-Room Trim
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you can only own one trim nailer, the right choice depends on what you are nailing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy a Finish Nailer First If</h3>

          <p>
            You are installing baseboard, door casing, window trim or crown in a full room. A 16-gauge finish nailer holds standard trim securely, and a 15-gauge model handles heavy doors and jambs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy a Brad Nailer First If</h3>

          <p>
            Your work is mostly small moldings, shoe mold, crafts and furniture. The <a href="/best-brad-nailer/">18-gauge brad nailers in our guide</a> leave smaller holes and are less likely to split thin pieces.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Many People End Up With Both</h3>

          <p>
            Trim carpenters typically use a finish nailer for the main trim and a brad nailer for the details. If you are also cutting the trim, a good <a href="/best-miter-saw/">miter saw</a> matters as much as either nailer for tight joints. Staying on one battery platform also makes tools like a <a href="/best-cordless-ratchet/">cordless ratchet</a> cheaper to add later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 15-Gauge for Delicate Trim</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fifteen-gauge nails hold best, but they leave bigger holes and split thin or narrow trim more easily. For shoe mold, small casing and cabinet trim, 16-gauge or a brad nailer is the better tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Nails Without Checking the Collation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Angled nails come in different angles and styles, and 15-gauge DA and FN nails are not interchangeable. Check the manual before buying, or the nails will misfeed and jam.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Scrap Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Depth that is perfect in pine can crush oak or leave nails proud in MDF. Fire a few test nails into an offcut of the same trim every time you change material.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Nailing Into Drywall Instead of Framing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Trim nailed only into drywall works loose. Mark the studs and plates first and aim every structural nail into wood.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting a New Battery Platform for One Nailer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bare cordless nailer is cheap if you already own the batteries and expensive if you do not. Buy the platform you use, choose a complete kit, or go pneumatic if you own a compressor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

