        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best finish nailer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the DeWalt DCN650B 20V 15-gauge angled finish nailer is the best finish nailer: cordless, with 15-gauge holding power and an angled magazine for corners. If you own a compressor, the Metabo HPT NT65MA4 pneumatic is lighter and faster. Buyers without batteries should look at the Metabo HPT NT1865DSA kit, and Milwaukee owners at the M18 FUEL Gen II.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I get a 15 or 16 gauge finish nailer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Get a 16-gauge finish nailer for most trim, including baseboard, window casing, chair rail and cabinets. The nails leave smaller holes and are less likely to split narrow trim. Choose 15-gauge for heavy door casing, jambs, stair treads and thick hardwood, where the thicker nail holds better. Every 15-gauge finish nailer is angled, so it also reaches corners well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an angled or straight finish nailer better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An angled finish nailer is better for corners, door jambs and crown molding near the ceiling, because the magazine tilts back out of the way. A straight finish nailer uses cheaper, more widely stocked 16-gauge nails and works well for baseboard and casing on open walls. If you do a lot of crown or doors, choose angled; for general baseboard, straight is fine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a finish nailer for baseboards?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Baseboard is the most common finish nailer job. A 16-gauge nailer with 2 to 2-1/2 inch nails driven into studs and the bottom plate holds most baseboard securely. For thick hardwood base or uneven walls, a 15-gauge nailer pulls the board tighter. Set the depth on scrap first so nail heads sit just below the surface for filling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cordless finish nailer as good as pneumatic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most DIYers, a cordless finish nailer is the better choice because there is no compressor or hose to manage. Pneumatic nailers are still lighter and fire faster, which matters on whole-house trim jobs. Some cordless nailers have a brief spin-up before firing, while newer designs like the Metabo HPT NT1865DSA list no ramp-up time and up to three nails per second.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a finish nailer use brad nails?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A finish nailer is built for 15- or 16-gauge nails, and 18-gauge brads are too thin to feed or drive correctly in it. You need a separate brad nailer for brads. Likewise, angled and straight nails are not interchangeable, and 15-gauge DA and FN style nails use different angles, so always buy the nail type your tool's manual specifies.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size nails do I need for trim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common rule is a nail about three times the thickness of the trim, long enough to pass through trim and drywall and bite into framing. In practice, thin casing nailed to a jamb takes around 1-1/4 to 1-1/2 inches, while baseboard and casing nailed into studs usually needs 2 to 2-1/2 inches. Keep a short and a long size on hand.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

